WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.319 --> 00:00:01.259
Good news, everyone!

2
00:00:01.539 --> 00:00:06.579
When you search Google, it doesn't look at the
Internet. It looks at a copy it already made.

3
00:00:06.899 --> 00:00:11.659
Somebody finally solved reading
everything, and it wasn't me.

4
00:00:12.099 --> 00:00:17.199
One machine has been out there for years
reading. It fetches a page, takes it

5
00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:22.139
apart, and writes down every
word and where it found it,

6
00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:24.199
not the page itself.

7
00:00:24.539 --> 00:00:27.419
A list of which words are on which pages.

8
00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:33.279
So by the time you type anything, the
work is already done. Your question

9
00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:35.359
never goes out to the Internet at all.

10
00:00:35.639 --> 00:00:41.119
It goes to that list. That's why the
answer arrives in less time than it

11
00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:43.739
takes you to lift your finger off the key.

12
00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:49.439
But a list of words isn't an answer
because you don't type what you

13
00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:54.299
mean. You type four words with a
spelling mistake in one of them.

14
00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:59.779
The second machine's entire job is
working out what you actually meant, and

15
00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:03.219
it has never fetched a page in its life.

16
00:01:03.559 --> 00:01:04.879
So there are two of them.

17
00:01:05.279 --> 00:01:10.360
One read everything and
understands nothing. The other

18
00:01:10.479 --> 00:01:13.779
understands you and has read nothing.

19
00:01:14.139 --> 00:01:15.860
Neither can do the other's job.

20
00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:21.199
Both have to be right, and they have to
finish before you get bored and leave.

21
00:01:21.559 --> 00:01:27.213
Marvellous, isn't it? Nobody
claps Comment index and I'll

22
00:01:27.252 --> 00:01:28.512
send you the diagram.

23
00:01:28.852 --> 00:01:30.592
Don't make me repeat myself
